Z||000000||hugh||11-04-2001||06:40 PM||||A few years ago,when I was trying to improve my Grand National I had thought of using some form of exhaust extractor similar to the what I thought the airlines used on their jet engines.I'll try to describe it.<BR>The last 10 or 15 inches of the exhaust would be cone shaped.Inside this cone would be another cone with the same shape so the 2 cones were identical .The Inside cone would be small enough so that it could be attached inside the larger cone with some form of struts.The area of the space between the cones would be the same as the exhaust pipe attached to the cones.they would both end on the sameplane,except the smaller cone would have a closed end with a very abreviated reverse cone.The thinking behind this design is the exhaust would lose no velocity entering the cone because of the same cross section area,but it's speed would increase as it left the cone.Any thoughts?||||reg||3
Z||000001||Mach10||11-04-2001||07:07 PM||||It would make a 5" fart can look a whole bunch more interesting, if nothing else  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Not sure that you'd be seeing much in the way of returns on this, though... The exhaust gasses would lose velocity throughout the whole system, from back-pressure from the muffler, to turbulence around bends/welds, to just plain old air resistance on the rough inner-surface. I'm not sure if the extractor will create enough of a vacuum to make much of a difference... ||||reg||10
